  "summary": "Recent preprints use correlation structure in open and monitored many-body dynamics to diagnose transitions, non-Markovianity and metrological gain.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is how correlation structure, often nonlocal or hidden, governs dynamics in open, monitored or symmetry-constrained quantum systems. On the mixed-state side, decoherence driving a 1D SPT state to a trivial product state reveals hidden long-range correlations, and hybrid measurement-unitary circuits show trajectory and projected-state ensembles undergoing ensemble transitions at the measurement-induced point. Non-Hermitian and dissipative structure appears in several forms: Lindbladian topological invariants read from covariance-matrix relaxation in Gaussian systems, channel-level exceptional points controlling the Markovian–non-Markovian boundary in a driven-dissipative Kerr-cat qubit, and reservoir engineering in Nambu space for exceptional-point sensing with quantum advantage. Symmetry dynamics form a related strand. Crosscap states show local symmetry breaking growing while entanglement decreases. Charged dual-unitary circuits with impurities show non-monotonic entropy growth. Z2-symmetric models link ground-state squeezing to collective excitation gaps. Gaussian ergotropy of detector pairs distinguishes accelerated vacuum from thermal fields.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-18"
